# =============================================================================
# Kenyon Express: environment variables
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development and fill in real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is committed and must never hold secrets.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined into the browser bundle at build time.
# Everything else stays server-side only. Never prefix a secret with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Legend:  [required]  the app throws or misbehaves without it
#          [optional]  feature is disabled / falls back when unset
#          [tooling]   only needed for scripts and local CLI work, not at runtime
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required] Project REST URL. Local stack: http://127.0.0.1:54321
# Used by: src/lib/supabase/{client,server,admin}.ts
#
# Trap, hit on 2026-07-28: pointing this at the local stack while that stack is
# not running does NOT raise. Every query returns empty, so the shop renders
# zero products and every product and category URL answers 404, with nothing in
# the log saying why. The local stack needs Docker; if `supabase status` cannot
# reach the daemon, point this at the hosted project instead of debugging the
# storefront.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# [required] Anon / publishable key. Public by design (RLS is what protects data).
# Used by: browser and SSR clients (src/lib/supabase/{client,server}.ts)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key

# [required] Service-role (secret) key. Bypasses RLS, so server-only. Never expose.
# src/lib/supabase/admin.ts reads SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY first and falls back
# to SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (the new-format name), so either one works. Set one.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# [tooling] Direct Postgres connection string. Not read at runtime.
# Used by: drizzle.config.ts (drizzle-kit) and scripts/db-doc.mjs
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@db.your-project-ref.supabase.co:5432/postgres


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required] Canonical public origin, no trailing slash. Drives SEO metadataBase
# (src/app/layout.tsx), Supabase auth redirect URLs (src/server/actions/auth.ts)
# and the Cardcom success / failure / webhook URLs. Must match the deployed
# domain or auth callbacks and payment returns break.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://kenyonexpress.co.il

# [optional] WhatsApp business number (05... or 972...). Empty hides all
# WhatsApp UI. Used by: src/lib/whatsapp.ts
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_PHONE=0501234567


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cardcom payment gateway (src/lib/payments/env.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In production ALL FOUR values below are required. loadCardcomEnv() throws
# `Missing required env: <NAME>` at request time otherwise. Outside production an
# unset CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER auto-selects the mock provider, so local dev and
# the test suite need none of these.

# The master switch for taking money, and it FAILS CLOSED: in production
# `src/lib/payments/env.ts` treats anything other than the exact string 'true'
# as off, including 'TRUE', '1' and unset. A production deploy that forgets this
# line serves a storefront that cannot check out, and it will not say why unless
# you know to look here. Locked in both directions by six tests.
CHECKOUT_ENABLED=

# [required in production] Cardcom terminal number.
CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER=

# [required in production] Cardcom API user name.
CARDCOM_API_NAME=

# [required in production] Cardcom API password.
CARDCOM_API_PASSWORD=

# [required in production] Long random string, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# Cardcom does NOT sign its callbacks, so this shared secret rides on the
# IndicatorUrl as `?s=` and is compared in constant time by the webhook route
# (src/app/api/payments/cardcom/webhook/route.ts). Treat it like a password.
CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# [optional] Force the in-memory mock provider ('true'). Implied when NODE_ENV
# is test, or in non-production with no CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER.
# CARDCOM_USE_MOCK=true

# [optional] Cardcom API host override. Default: https://secure.cardcom.solutions
# CARDCOM_API_BASE_URL=

# [optional] Kill switch. Exactly 'false' rejects new checkouts while leaving the
# rest of the site up. Any other value (or unset) means enabled.
# CHECKOUT_ENABLED=false


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 image storage (src/lib/storage/r2.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [optional] All five must be set together; if any is missing the image pipeline
# silently falls back to Supabase Storage. The R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL hostname must
# also be allowed in next.config.ts `images.remotePatterns`.
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.kenyonexpress.co.il


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meilisearch (src/lib/search-server.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [optional] HOST and API_KEY must both be set to enable Meilisearch; otherwise
# search falls back to a Postgres ILIKE query. INDEX defaults to 'products'.
# MEILISEARCH_HOST=https://search.kenyonexpress.co.il
# MEILISEARCH_API_KEY=
# MEILISEARCH_INDEX=products


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search index pipeline (src/lib/search/{pipeline-contracts,qstash,indexer}.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required for live indexing] Shared secret for the Supabase Database Webhook
# on public.products -> /api/webhooks/products. `openssl rand -hex 32`. The
# sender either signs the body (x-search-signature: hex HMAC-SHA256) or sends
# the secret itself (x-webhook-secret) — Supabase dashboard webhooks can only
# attach static headers, so they use the second form.
SEARCH_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# [optional] Upstash QStash queue (retry + DLQ) between the webhook and the
# index worker. When TOKEN is unset, jobs run inline in the webhook request —
# fine for dev and preview, not for production bursts. The two signing keys
# verify the Upstash-Signature header on /api/search/index-job and
# /api/search/index-dlq; both are needed (Upstash rotates current -> next).
# QSTASH_TOKEN=
# QSTASH_CURRENT_SIGNING_KEY=
# QSTASH_NEXT_SIGNING_KEY=

# [required] Bearer secret for cron routes AND manual DLQ replays against
# /api/search/index-job. Vercel Cron sends it automatically once set.
# (Already used by /api/cron/expire-vouchers.)
CRON_SECRET=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform-provided (do not set by hand)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERCEL_URL: injected per deployment by Vercel.
# CI: set by the CI runner; makes Playwright fail on .only, retry twice
#               and run single-worker (playwright.config.ts).


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local scripts only (scripts/screenshot-all.mjs, scripts/compare.mjs)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visual-diff helpers comparing the local build against the live WordPress site.
# Never needed by the app or by CI.
# LIVE_BASE=https://kenyonexpress.co.il
# LOCAL_BASE=http://localhost:3000
# LIVE_PRODUCT_PATH=/product/example
# LOCAL_PRODUCT_PATH=/product/example
# LIVE_CATEGORY_PATH=/product-category/example
# LOCAL_CATEGORY_PATH=/category/example
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Voucher QR signing
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [required] HMAC secret for voucher QR issue/redeem. 32+ random bytes
# (base64 or hex). No default is ever used: a missing secret is a hard
# failure at sign and verify time.
VOUCHER_QR_SECRET=

# [optional] The prior secret, accepted on verify only, for a zero-downtime rotation.
VOUCHER_QR_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# [optional] Key id embedded in signed payloads. Default v1.
VOUCHER_QR_KEY_ID=v1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cardcom (Phase-2: required only when CHECKOUT_ENABLED=true in production)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional] Override the API base. Default is the production endpoint;
# point at the sandbox terminal for testing.
CARDCOM_API_BASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 (image pipeline; falls back to Supabase Storage when unset)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional] All five must be set together for R2 uploads.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meilisearch (search; /search falls back to Postgres LIKE when unset)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional] All three go together.
MEILISEARCH_HOST=
MEILISEARCH_API_KEY=
MEILISEARCH_INDEX=products


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WordPress import (tooling only; never needed at runtime)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [tooling] WooCommerce REST credentials for scripts/wp-import.
WC_BASE=
WC_KEY=
WC_SECRET=

# [tooling] The ETL is dry-run by default. Writes happen only when this is
# literally "true" AND the run targets the local stack.
WP_IMPORT_ALLOW_WRITES=

# [tooling] Server-side Supabase URL for scripts that run outside Next.js
# (falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL when unset).
SUPABASE_URL=

# [optional] New-format name for the service key; admin.ts accepts either this
# or SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# Observability lives in its own block further down: the money-path-only
# description that used to sit here stopped being true when instrumentation.ts
# started capturing every server error and left only the PHONE narrow.

# ── Cardcom multi-account ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The platform account comes from CARDCOM_TERMINAL_NUMBER / CARDCOM_API_NAME /
# CARDCOM_API_PASSWORD above. Extra accounts (an anchor supplier clearing on its
# own terminal) go here as a JSON array:
#   [{"id":"anchor-x","terminalNumber":"990001","apiName":"...","apiPassword":"..."}]
CARDCOM_ACCOUNTS=
# Marks the platform account as test credentials. Terminal 1000 is treated as
# sandbox regardless, since it is Cardcom's shared test terminal.
CARDCOM_SANDBOX=
# Escape hatch: allows sandbox credentials to load under NODE_ENV=production,
# for a production-like staging deploy. Without it the app refuses to start.
CARDCOM_ALLOW_SANDBOX=

# [local only] `next start` on a laptop is NODE_ENV=production, which is how the
# Playwright suite and the Lighthouse runs measure the real build. Set this to
# "true" there so src/lib/env.ts does not refuse to boot over Cardcom secrets a
# laptop has no business holding. It prints a warning every time it is honoured.
# NEVER set it anywhere a customer can reach: it is the one thing standing
# between a missing secret and a checkout that cannot charge.
ALLOW_INCOMPLETE_ENV=

# Outbound email (Resend). Absent means email is disabled, which is the correct
# state for local development and CI: a checkout must not fail because nobody
# configured mail on a laptop. EMAIL_FROM must be a domain Resend has verified.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM="KenyonExpress <noreply@kenyonexpress.co.il>"
# [optional] Inbox for /contact form submissions. Defaults to info@kenyonexpress.co.il.
CONTACT_TO=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sentry + alerting (src/lib/observability/)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ORG IS IN THE EU REGION. Every DSN below must be on the host
# `ingest.de.sentry.io`, and sentry-cli / the API must be pointed at
# https://de.sentry.io. Against the default US host this org 404s, which looks
# like a bad token and is not. Project: kenyonexpress/kenyonexpress-web.
#
# [optional] Server DSN. Absent = Sentry is entirely inert: no init, no network
# call, nothing queued. That is what keeps CI and local dev credential-free.
SENTRY_DSN=
# [optional] Browser DSN. Separate from the server one so a client key that ends
# up in the bundle (it must, to work) is not the server key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=development
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=development
# [CI] Source-map upload. Without SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN the build skips the upload
# silently rather than failing, so a fork's CI stays green.
#
# SILENTLY IS THE PROBLEM ON A REAL DEPLOY. Production JS is minified, so an
# unuploaded map means every production stack trace is a column offset into a
# one-line chunk. `pnpm sentry:verify` fails when the token is missing, so the
# gap is asserted somewhere rather than only being quiet in a build log.
#
# The token is org-scoped and can CREATE AND DELETE PROJECTS. It belongs in
# Vercel env (all three environments), never in a committed file and not on a
# laptop. Scopes needed: project:releases, org:read.
SENTRY_ORG=kenyonexpress
SENTRY_PROJECT=kenyonexpress-web
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# [optional] Release id. CI sets both to the commit sha; they MUST match or the
# maps are attached to a release nothing reports against.
SENTRY_RELEASE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_RELEASE=
# [optional] Push alerts. Only the money path reaches this, by design.
NTFY_TOPIC=kenyon-ofir-limit
NTFY_BASE_URL=https://ntfy.sh
# [optional] Set to "false" to silence the phone without redeploying code.
ALERTS_ENABLED=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Growth: newsletter + abandoned cart (src/lib/growth/)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shares RESEND_API_KEY with the transactional sender above; absent means every
# growth send returns `skipped` and nothing is mailed, which is what lets local
# dev and CI run these paths with no credential.
# [optional] Marketing sender identity. Falls back to EMAIL_FROM when unset, so
# one verified domain configures both senders. Must be verified in Resend.
RESEND_FROM=
# [optional] Audience to mirror consent into. Our DB stays the source of truth.
RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=
# [optional] Hours a cart must be idle before it is nudged. Default 3.
ABANDONED_CART_HOURS=3
# [required in production] Salt for hashing consent IPs. Consent evidence needs
# equality, not the address, so the raw IP is never stored.
CONSENT_IP_SALT=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wallet passes: Apple Wallet + Google Wallet (src/lib/wallet/)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per platform, and ALL-OR-NOTHING per platform. A partially configured platform
# is treated as unconfigured: the coupon page renders no button for it, and
# /api/wallet/apple/[id] answers 404. A button that returns 500 is worse than no
# button, because the customer cannot tell it apart from a broken coupon page.
#
# The certificates are read as base64 OR as a PEM pasted directly. Vercel has no
# filesystem to put a .p12 on, and a PEM in an env var loses its newlines in
# half the tools that touch it on the way.
#
# [optional] Apple. Needs a Pass Type ID certificate from the Apple Developer
# portal plus the Apple WWDR intermediate, which the phone does not go and fetch.
APPLE_WALLET_PASS_TYPE_ID=
APPLE_WALLET_TEAM_ID=
APPLE_WALLET_CERT_PEM=
APPLE_WALLET_KEY_PEM=
APPLE_WALLET_WWDR_PEM=
# [optional] Only when the private key above is encrypted.
APPLE_WALLET_KEY_PASSPHRASE=
# [optional] Shown as the pass issuer. Defaults to KenyonExpress.
APPLE_WALLET_ORG_NAME=
#
# [optional] Google. Needs an Issuer account and a service account with the
# wallet_object.issuer scope. Saving is a signed link and makes no API call;
# only the redemption push does, and it fails silently by design.
GOOGLE_WALLET_ISSUER_ID=
GOOGLE_WALLET_SA_EMAIL=
GOOGLE_WALLET_SA_KEY_PEM=
# [optional] Class the voucher objects belong to. Default kenyon_voucher.
GOOGLE_WALLET_CLASS_SUFFIX=

# [optional] The Cardcom webhook secret being RETIRED, accepted on the way in
# and never handed out. Rotation needs two: `?s=<secret>` is the only thing
# authenticating a callback (Cardcom signs nothing), and the secret is baked into
# the IndicatorUrl when the payment page is created — so at any instant there are
# live payment pages carrying the old one. Replacing a single value drops every
# one of those callbacks, and drops them with a 200, so Cardcom never retries.
# Rotation: set PREVIOUS to the current value, set the current to the new one,
# remove PREVIOUS once the longest checkout has expired.
CARDCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications (mobile app, migration 114)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OFF UNLESS EXPLICITLY ON. With this unset the notification drain settles every
# push leg as `skipped` WITHOUT counting an attempt, so turning it on later does
# not find a queue whose five retries were already spent on a machine that was
# never meant to notify anyone. A preview deployment must leave it unset.
PUSH_ENABLED=
#
# [optional] Expo accepts unauthenticated sends for most projects, so an empty
# value here is normal and is NOT the switch. Set it only if the Expo project is
# configured to require an access token; then it is a secret.
EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phone sign-in (SMS OTP)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE FLAG IS NOT THE PROVIDER. Supabase only sends an SMS when a phone provider
# (Twilio Verify, in this project's plan) is wired into the project's Auth
# settings, and that is a dashboard action no code here can take. This variable
# only says whether that has been done: with it unset, the SMS option is hidden
# from the login page entirely rather than shown as a button that always fails
# with a message the customer cannot act on.
#
# ORDER OF OPERATIONS: configure Twilio in the Supabase dashboard first, send
# one real test code, and only then set this to true.
PHONE_AUTH_ENABLED=
#
# The same switch for the mobile app's bundle (apps/mobile). Deliberately a
# separate variable: the server one must not be inlined into any client bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PHONE_AUTH_ENABLED=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cardcom document module (invoices, migration 116)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE NUMERIC CODES CANNOT BE VERIFIED FROM THIS MACHINE, which is why they are
# variables and not constants. There are no CARDCOM_* credentials here and the
# only document in the repo describes the v11 JSON API, while the live client is
# the legacy /Interface/*.aspx one. What IS certain is WHICH document is being
# asked for; only the provider's code for it may need correcting against a real
# terminal, and these let that happen without a deploy.
#
# A sale deliberately sends NO InvoiceType at all, so the terminal's own default
# (tax invoice + receipt) applies. Only these two override it.
#
# [optional] Cardcom's code for a credit note. Default 3.
CARDCOM_CREDIT_NOTE_TYPE=
# [optional] Cardcom's code for a plain receipt, used for coupon-only orders:
# a coupon's payment is an ADVANCE and the VAT event has not happened yet, so
# the document states no VAT. Default 4.
CARDCOM_COUPON_RECEIPT_TYPE=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GA4 and the Meta Pixel
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NOTHING LOADS BEFORE CONSENT, whatever is set here. `ThirdPartyTags` renders
# no script tag at all until the visitor accepts - not even a consent-mode-denied
# bootstrap, which is what Google's own guide recommends. The reasoning is in
# lib/analytics/third-party.ts: fetching the script already hands Google the
# visitor's IP, and that transfer is the thing consent is for.
#
# THE TWO NEXT_PUBLIC IDs ARE NOT SECRETS. They are visible in any page that
# loads them. They are public because they must reach the browser.
#
# A MALFORMED ID DISABLES THE TAG rather than loading it. A Tag Manager
# container (GTM-) or a Google Ads id (AW-) pasted into the GA4 variable loads
# without error and measures nothing, which is the worst failure there is.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=
#
# THESE TWO ARE SECRETS, and they are what makes the SERVER-side purchase work.
# A browser purchase event is lost every time a tab closes on the payment
# redirect and every time an ad blocker eats it; `finalizeOrder` reports the
# authoritative one over these APIs, deduplicated on the order id.
#
# GA4: Admin -> Data Streams -> your stream -> Measurement Protocol API secrets.
GA4_API_SECRET=
# Meta: Events Manager -> your pixel -> Settings -> Conversions API access token.
META_CAPI_TOKEN=
